window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][94] = "<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes 30 seconds).</strong> &quot;In my first launch as lead analyst, I disagreed with a senior designer about our onboarding flow. It had six steps and only 41% of new users finished. I thought two of the steps were costing us completions; she thought each step mattered. My choice was to argue harder, go to the product manager, or find a way to test it. I chose to test, because her point was plausible and I only had correlations. First I asked her what each step was for and wrote it down.</p>\n<p>That showed that two steps collected data for a feature only 9% of users reached. I proposed an experiment moving those two steps to after first use, and we agreed together on the metrics: completion as the goal, and seven-day activation as a guardrail. She suggested a change to the copy, which I added. Completion rose from 41% to 47%, activation was flat, and we shipped it. About a point of that lift came from her copy change. What I took from it is to ask what a step is for before deciding it is the problem, and to agree the guardrail with the person I disagree with before the test.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Signals.</strong> Respect for the other view (asked, wrote it down, used her idea); a real decision (test, not escalate); evidence with limits (&quot;the interval included zero&quot;); credit given; learning that is behavioral. A weaker version: &quot;We disagreed, I showed her the data, and she agreed.&quot; It would give no decision, no action and no learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Probes to prepare.</strong> What was the sample size and duration? What if activation had dropped? How did you feel when she pushed back? Did the result change her mind?</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The decision part (why test) is what makes it a PM story, and crediting the colleague makes the conflict credible.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-3-a-failure-story-and-the-probe-ladder-illust\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — A failure story and the probe ladder (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt (invented story).</strong> &quot;Tell me about a decision you got wrong.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>The failure-story test (FW-C31-06).</strong> <em>Real cost:</em> a roadmap decision was delayed two weeks (invented) and a stakeholder lost some trust in my numbers. <em>My decision:</em> I reported a result without reconciling it. <em>Honest reason it seemed right:</em> the lift was large and it matched what the team expected. <em>Repair:</em> I told the stakeholder the same day I found the error, reran the analysis, sent a correction. <em>Behavior change:</em> I now reconcile any headline number against an independent source before sharing, and I have a short checklist.</p>\n<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes 30 seconds).</strong> &quot;Early in my current job I told the team that a new notification design had raised retention by 6 points. It was a big number and it matched what everyone hoped, and I shared it in a team meeting without checking it against anything else. Two days later I was preparing the readout for the director and noticed that the treatment group's events were counted twice because of a logging change that shipped with the test. After correcting it, the lift was 1.1 points. I told my manager and the product manager that afternoon, sent a written correction, and reran the analysis with the engineer who owned the logging. The cost was real: a roadmap decision that had leaned on the 6 points was delayed by two weeks, and a stakeholder told me she would now want a second pair of eyes on my numbers. What I do differently is reconcile any headline figure against a second source before I share it, and I check event counts per user between groups. I used it last quarter, when a dashboard figure didn't match finance's; I found a definition difference before it went into a slide.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Probe ladder (FW-C31-05).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Level 1, detail.</em> &quot;What exactly was wrong in the logging?&quot; — &quot;A new event fired once on the server and once on the client in the treatment arm; I found it by comparing events per user between arms, which were 1.9 times higher in treatment.&quot; &quot;How did you tell people?&quot; — &quot;In person to my manager first, then a written note to the thread with the corrected number and the cause.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 2, reasoning.</em> &quot;Why did you share before checking?&quot; — &quot;The number matched our hopes and I was keen to give good news; I treated plausibility as verification. I also didn't want to hold up a meeting.&quot; &quot;How did you decide the corrected number was right?&quot; — &quot;Two independent counts agreed after the fix, and the engineer confirmed the event schema.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 3, counterfactual and limits.</em> &quot;Would the decision have been different if the true figure had been known?&quot; — &quot;Probably the same direction but slower: 1.1 points was still positive, but smaller than the cost of one of the options, so they would have chosen differently.&quot; &quot;Is there anything you still don't know?&quot; — &quot;Whether the logging error affected earlier tests; I checked the last three and found none.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A failure with a real cost, an honest reason, a repair and a habit with evidence that it is used.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-4-building-a-story-bank-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Building a story bank (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The inventory (FW-C31-04).</strong> A candidate with about two years of experience lists 12 episodes (invented): (1) the first funnel dashboard and form cut; (2) the notification default experiment; (3) the onboarding disagreement with a designer; (4) the double-counted events error; (5) a user-interview project that changed a feature's scope; (6) a launch that missed its date after an estimate was cut by management; (7) mentoring a new analyst; (8) a campus hackathon app with a team of four; (9) pushing back on a request to use a pre-checked subscription box; (10) a pricing-page test that failed; (11) helping support build a report that cut ticket time; (12) a quarter in which three requests competed for one engineer.</p>\n<p><strong>Selection.</strong> Keep episodes with a real decision, clear stakes, an outcome, a learning and a story you can tell truthfully for ten minutes. Drop (7) (little decision), (11) (mostly someone else's work) and (8) (too old and too far from the role), keeping 9.</p>\n<p><strong>Mapping to themes.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Theme</th>\n<th>Primary story</th>\n<th>Backup</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>(3) the onboarding disagreement</td>\n<td>(6) the missed date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>(4) the double-counted events</td>\n<td>(10) the failed pricing test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Influence without authority</td>\n<td>(2) the notification default</td>\n<td>(1) the form cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ambiguity</td>\n<td>(5) the user-interview project</td>\n<td>(12) the three requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer focus</td>\n<td>(5) the user-interview project</td>\n<td>(1) the form cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data-driven decision</td>\n<td>(2) the notification default</td>\n<td>(10) the failed pricing test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prioritization</td>\n<td>(12) the three competing requests</td>\n<td>(6) the missed date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leading others</td>\n<td>(6) the launch, where I coordinated four people</td>\n<td>(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Values and judgment</td>\n<td>(9) the pre-checked box</td>\n<td>none yet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Coverage check.</strong> Nine themes and nine stories, with some stories carrying two themes. Gaps: no backup for values (develop (9) with a second angle, and look for a second episode); (5) carries two themes, so choose which to lead with in a given loop. No story appears more than twice as a primary.</p>\n<p><strong>Use the attribute map (FW-C31-01).</strong> For a loop at a company that publishes a leadership list including ownership, disagreement and commitment, and results (Amazon-type), lead with (3), (4) and (2); for a company stressing growth mindset (Microsoft-type), lead with (4), (10) and (5). The stories do not change; the order and emphasis do.</p>\n<p><strong>Write notes, not scripts.</strong> Each of the nine stories gets five lines (context, decision, action, result, learning), the key numbers, two probes with answers, and what to omit.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Nine well-prepared stories cover the themes, and the bank is organized by decision and evidence, not by job title.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-5-a-values-scenario-pressure-to-hit-a-number-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A values scenario: pressure to hit a number (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Your manager wants to hit this quarter's sign-up target by pre-checking a box that subscribes people to a weekly email. You think it is manipulative. What do you do?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Apply the frame (FW-C31-08).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Facts.</em> What does the data say? A small test (invented): with the box pre-checked, sign-ups per 1,000 visitors rise from 80 to 101 (from 8.0% to 10.1%), but 7-day unsubscribes rise from 12% to 31%, and complaints triple. I also check the law and company policy: consent rules in several regions require opt-in for marketing email <code>[V: GDPR Article 4(11) (clear affirmative action) and the ePrivacy Directive (opt-in for marketing email), as summarized in guidance]</code>.</li>\n<li><em>Stakeholders and harms.</em> Users who did not intend to subscribe (annoyance, loss of trust); the company (complaints, spam reports, regulatory risk); the team's credibility.</li>\n<li><em>The pressure and the legitimate goal.</em> The target is a growth number; the legitimate goal is more engaged subscribers.</li>\n<li><em>The real effect.</em> Retained subscribers per 1,000 visitors after a week: before, 80 × (1 − 0.12) = 70.4; after, 101 × (1 − 0.31) = 69.7. The tactic produces no net gain and adds complaints and risk. It raises the number that is reported and not the number that matters (Goodhart, C05).</li>\n<li><em>Options.</em> Comply; refuse; propose an opt-in design that improves the value proposition (show a sample email, explain the benefit), test it against the pre-checked version on retained subscribers and complaints, and report the retained-subscribers measure to leadership.</li>\n<li><em>Principle.</em> A default that works against users' stated interests is a dark pattern (C10.10).</li>\n<li><em>Action and escalation.</em> Talk to my manager first, privately, with the numbers; propose the test and a revised target definition (retained subscribers); if told to ship it anyway and it appears to violate policy or law, raise it with legal or the policy owner, in writing, and decline to implement what I believe is unlawful.</li>\n<li><em>Cost I accept.</em> A harder conversation, a possible missed sign-up target this quarter, and the risk of being seen as difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes).</strong> &quot;I would first check the facts, then talk to my manager with the numbers. In a small test, pre-checking the box raised sign-ups per thousand visitors from 80 to 101, but unsubscribes within a week went from 12% to 31%, so after a week we kept about 70 subscribers per thousand either way, with three times the complaints. So the tactic moves the number we report and not the number we want. I would propose an opt-in version that shows a sample of the email, test it against the pre-checked one on retained subscribers and complaints, and suggest we measure the target on retained subscribers. If I were told to ship the pre-checked box anyway and I believed it broke consent rules, I would raise it with legal in writing and not build it. It could cost us the sign-up target this quarter and some goodwill with my manager, and I would accept that.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The answer does not preach: it finds the legitimate goal, measures the real effect, offers a better route and states the cost.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Tell me about a time you disagreed with your manager.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;Once my manager wanted to launch a feature and I thought we should wait. We talked about it, and in the end we found a compromise that worked for everyone. It taught me the importance of communication.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no context or stakes, no decision, no action, no result, no number; a generic learning. It gives the interviewer nothing to probe.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;Last year my manager wanted to launch a redesigned checkout in the same week as a pricing change. I was concerned that we could not tell which change caused any movement in conversion, and that a problem would be hard to diagnose. I raised it in our one-to-one with a specific example from an earlier launch where two changes overlapped and we spent two weeks untangling the result. She worried about missing a seasonal window. We agreed to stagger the launches by a week, with the checkout first, which cost her four days against the window. Conversion rose 2.1% on the checkout alone, and the pricing change a week later was read cleanly. What I took from it is to bring a concrete example and a proposal, not only a concern.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I want to be honest that I was partly wrong. When I raised it, I assumed the seasonal window was flexible. My manager showed me it was worth about 8% of the quarter's volume, and that changed my proposal: instead of delaying the pricing change by a week, we launched it to a tenth of users the same week so that we captured most of the window while keeping a clean read from the other nine tenths. That was her idea built on my concern. When we disagreed over the final design, she decided, and I committed: I helped write the launch plan even though I preferred a longer delay.</p>\n<p>&quot;The result was a clean read of both changes and 90% of the window's volume. What I changed afterward is that I now ask what a deadline is worth in numbers before I argue against it, and I agree in advance what evidence would make us reverse. When she asked what I would do differently, I said I would have brought the cost of the delay in numbers to the first conversation, not discovered it in the second.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Names stakes, a decision, an action and a number</td>\n<td>Admits where you were partly wrong and uses the other person's idea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives a concrete example and a proposal</td>\n<td>Shows commitment after the decision went the other way</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers a learning</td>\n<td>Gives a behavioral change and its use since</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats and published values change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes sixteen Leadership Principles on its careers site and describes using them every day <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles, as cited by several summaries]</code>. Candidates commonly report that behavioral questions probe these principles, often with several rounds and detailed follow-ups <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: prepare eight to ten stories with numbers and your personal decisions (ownership, dive deep, disagreeing and committing, delivering results), and expect repeated probing for detail; do not recite principle names.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google describes assessing general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and an attribute called Googleyness (not to be confused with culture fit, by Google's own clarification) in its hiring materials <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>. Candidates commonly report structured behavioral questions about ambiguity, collaboration and leadership without authority <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: choose stories with unclear goals and several stakeholders, show how you structured the problem and brought others along, and stay humble about what you did not know.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's published values emphasize moving fast, long-term impact and direct communication <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta's careers culture page, six core values]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about impact, speed and tradeoffs, and about giving and receiving direct feedback <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with results and numbers, show a speed-quality tradeoff you made and what it cost, and give an example of direct, respectful candor.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's culture materials emphasize a growth mindset (learning and curiosity over knowing everything) and collaboration <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about learning from mistakes, working across teams and customer focus <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: include stories in which you changed your mind or learned something that changed your approach, and show how you helped others learn.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple does not publish a hiring rubric; candidates commonly report an emphasis on craft, attention to detail, collaboration across disciplines and discretion <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: have a story about obsessing over a detail and its cost, one about protecting users or privacy against a business request, and be careful not to disclose confidential information from past employers in any story.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes a culture memo (last updated June 2024) built on four principles, with candor among its values and managers expected to give people context <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on independent judgment, candid feedback and decisions made without waiting for approval <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: prepare a story where you gave candid feedback to someone more senior, and one where you made a call on your own judgment and owned the outcome.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe publishes operating principles that include users first and moving with urgency and focus <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe careers pages, principles list; also quoted by The Pragmatic Engineer and Aced]</code>, and is widely described as valuing clear thinking and writing <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Candidates report behavioral questions on rigor and care for users, especially developers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show stories with precise reasoning and care about the user's real problem, and be ready to explain a decision clearly in writing-style structure.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c31-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A rambling introduction</td>\n<td>Four minutes, no arc</td>\n<td>Present, past in steps with reasons, future, hand over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A generic motive for PM</td>\n<td>&quot;I like solving problems&quot;</td>\n<td>Evidence of what drew you and what you learned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>All &quot;we&quot;</td>\n<td>The interviewer cannot tell what you did</td>\n<td>&quot;I&quot; for your decisions and actions; credit others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>No decision</td>\n<td>A story that is a sequence of events</td>\n<td>State the options and why you chose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>No numbers</td>\n<td>&quot;It went well&quot;</td>\n<td>A figure with a baseline, or an honest &quot;I don't have it&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A fake failure or weakness</td>\n<td>&quot;I work too hard&quot;</td>\n<td>A real cost, an owned decision, a changed habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Reciting company principles</td>\n<td>&quot;That shows ownership&quot;</td>\n<td>Tell the story; let them label it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Inventing detail</td>\n<td>A number that changes under probing</td>\n<td>Use only what is true; say what you don't recall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Defensiveness under probing</td>\n<td>Arguing with the follow-up</td>\n<td>Acknowledge the flaw; say what you now think</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Answering what you want to tell</td>\n<td>A story for a different theme</td>\n<td>Answer the question asked; say so if you pivot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> and <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> are from C02, and the pre-mortem (<a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>) is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Steelmanning</strong> (<code>MM-steelman</code>). State the other side's position in its strongest form before responding. In a conflict story it shows respect and understanding: &quot;Her point was that each step helps later use, which was plausible,&quot; and it makes your resolution credible.</p>\n<p><strong>Hanlon's razor</strong> (<code>MM-hanlon</code>). Do not attribute to malice what is adequately explained by misunderstanding or competing constraints <code>[V: Robert J. Hanlon, in Arthur Bloch, Murphy's Law Book Two, 1980, page 52; earlier similar lines: Heinlein, Logic of Empire, 1941; Goethe, 1774. The book wording is an adaptation of the original, which says stupidity]</code>. Tell conflict stories as differences in information and incentives, not villains.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To prepare, ask how an answer would fail: no decision, no number, all &quot;we,&quot; a probe you cannot answer. Then fix each in the story.</p>\n<p><strong>The pre-mortem</strong> (<code>MM-pre-mortem</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>, C02). For a values scenario, imagine the decision was regretted a year later and ask why; the answers (a missing fact, a harm you ignored) are what to check now.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<p>Your stories involve other people, and they will describe the same events differently. Test each story against how they would tell it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> An engineer in your story would care about what you asked of them and whether you listened to the constraint. If you say you &quot;convinced engineering,&quot; ask whether they would say they were persuaded or pushed. Credit their contribution in the story.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> A designer would care about whether you took their reasoning seriously. Tell conflict stories so that the designer would recognize their own view in your account of it.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> A data scientist would check the numbers and the claim about causation. Be precise about what the data showed and what it did not.</li>\n<li><strong>Your manager.</strong> A manager would check scope and credit: did you do this, or did you only report it? Do not claim a decision that was your manager's; describe your part.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c31-checklist-before-you-walk-into-a-behavioral-round\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you walk into a behavioral round</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Do I have a two-minute introduction with a clear arc and three proof points I can expand on?</li>\n<li>Do I have honest, specific reasons for product management and for this company and role?</li>\n<li>Do I have eight to ten stories, each with notes on context, decision, action, result and learning, and verified numbers?</li>\n<li>Does my bank cover conflict, failure, influence, ambiguity, customer focus, a data-driven decision, prioritization, leading and a values moment?</li>\n<li>For each story, can I say what I did, as opposed to what the team did?</li>\n<li>Have I prepared two probes at each of three levels for every story, and one thing that went wrong?</li>\n<li>Do I have a real failure, a real weakness and a values story that cost something?</li>\n<li>Have I rehearsed aloud, timed to two to three minutes, and mapped stories to the company's published attributes without reciting them?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c31-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c31-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C31-01 · The answer that said &quot;we&quot; forty times · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A candidate with two years of experience practices the prompt &quot;Tell me about a project you led&quot; with a friend who has interviewed candidates. The candidate talks for five minutes about a redesign of a reporting dashboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A project the candidate cared about; a habit of describing team outcomes; no practice at timing.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The friend counts: &quot;we&quot; appears about 40 times and &quot;I&quot; six times; there is no number in the story; the friend cannot say what the candidate decided; the story's learning is &quot;teamwork is important.&quot; The friend's note: &quot;I don't know if you led it or watched it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The story contains no decision, no personal action and no result, so the listener can infer nothing about the candidate's judgment.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The content was probably good. The telling hid it. Using the anatomy (FW-C31-03): <em>Context</em> took three minutes; <em>Decision</em> was missing; <em>Action</em> was narrated as &quot;we&quot;; <em>Result</em> had no number; <em>Learning</em> was generic. The probe test: ask &quot;what did you personally decide?&quot; and &quot;what was the result?&quot;; the candidate answers both well when asked, which shows the material exists. The rewrite: two sentences of context (the dashboard took four minutes to load and 60% of managers stopped using it); the decision (rebuild everything or cut it to five key charts, and I chose to cut, after interviewing six managers); the action (the interviews, the proposal, getting the data team to build a cached view); the result (load time to 8 seconds, weekly use from 40% to 71% of managers); the learning (talk to users before choosing scope).</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep the story as it is</td>\n<td>Comfort</td>\n<td>No signal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Rewrite with the five parts and numbers, and rehearse to three minutes</td>\n<td>Signal</td>\n<td>Work to recall the numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Switch to a different story</td>\n<td>Possibly a better story</td>\n<td>Loses the stronger material</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs an evening and turns a good project into a good story; C is unnecessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Write the five-part notes, replace each &quot;we&quot; with the specific &quot;I&quot; or a named role where true, add the baseline and result numbers, and time it at 2 minutes 40 seconds. Prepare two probes at each level.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the rewritten story ran 2 minutes 40 seconds with 14 uses of &quot;I&quot; for personal actions and 9 of &quot;we&quot; for team results, included the baseline and the result numbers, and survived five minutes of probing by the friend (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the candidate had in fact made no decisions on the project, the honest choice would have been a different story. A made-up decision would have collapsed under the probe &quot;why not rebuild everything?&quot; The lesson: a story is evidence of what you did; tell the decision, the action and the number, and use only what is true.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c31-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C31-02 · Johnson &amp; Johnson, 1982: a values decision with a known cost · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): a contemporaneous Fortune article, &quot;The fight to save Tylenol&quot; by Thomas Moore (29 November 1982), read in full; the Food and Drug Administration's compliance policy guide on tamper-resistant packaging (CPG 450.500) and the Federal Register notices of the 1982 rule; a university ethics case study of the recall (University of New Mexico, Daniels Fund Ethics Initiative), for later share figures and the company's Credo; and educational and journalistic summaries for the 1983 anti-tampering law. Sources differ on some figures (noted below), and none gives the internal deliberations in detail. The analysis is the author's. The case is used to show the shape of a values decision, not to praise or judge a company. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In late September 1982 seven people in the Chicago area died after taking Extra-Strength Tylenol capsules that had been laced with cyanide, in what investigators concluded was tampering after the bottles reached store shelves, since the poisoned bottles came from different factories. Tylenol, made by a Johnson &amp; Johnson subsidiary, was a leading pain reliever, with a market share reported as about 35 to 37 percent, and by one account accounted for 15 to 20 percent of the company's profits. Johnson &amp; Johnson's chairman, James Burke, took charge of the response personally, met the FBI and the FDA in Washington on the Monday after the first weekend, and, on the Monday after the second weekend, formed a strategic group of key executives to oversee the work of rebuilding the brand <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>. The company used the media to warn the public and urged people to switch to tablets <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>. Burke began to advocate recalling all Extra-Strength capsules, but the FBI and the FDA counseled against a precipitous recall: the FBI feared it would tell the perpetrator he was winning, and the FDA feared it would cause more public anxiety than it would relieve <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>.</p>\n<p>On the following Tuesday (5 October, according to reference summaries), after what appeared to be a copycat strychnine poisoning in California, the FDA agreed with Burke that all Tylenol capsules should be recalled nationwide: about 31 million bottles with a retail value of over $100 million, a cost the company estimated at about $100 million before taxes, which cut third-quarter earnings per share from 78 cents the year before to 51 cents (Fortune). By late November 1982 the company had a new tamper-resistant package and was preparing to bring capsules back, and sales of Tylenol products had initially fallen about 80% <code>[V: Fortune, 29 November 1982]</code>; accounts differ on exactly when the product returned to the shelves. Tylenol's share is reported to have fallen from 37% of the over-the-counter pain reliever market to about 7% by late 1982 and to have recovered to about 30% within six months (the University of New Mexico case study), while other accounts give 8% and about a year <code>[V: UNM case study; secondary accounts]</code>. The FDA published final tamper-resistant packaging regulations for most over-the-counter drugs on 5 November 1982, about five weeks after the first deaths, and amended them in 1989 to require two tamper-resistant features for two-piece capsules unless they are sealed by a tamper-resistant technology <code>[V: FDA CPG 450.500; Federal Register]</code>. Congress passed an anti-tampering law in 1983, making product tampering a federal crime <code>[V: reference summaries]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A product central to the company's profits and identity; uncertainty about whether the contamination was local or national, and who was responsible; public fear; a media environment moving by the hour; a company credo that put customers first, which the committee used as a guide, according to later case studies (Fortune's contemporaneous account does not mention it); and the fact that the poisoning occurred after the product left the company's control, which would have allowed a narrower response.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the deaths, the share and profit figures, the recall's scale and cost, the earnings effect, the share recovery, the packaging change and the rule change. Accounts differ on the exact share figures (Fortune: a 35% share of a $1 billion analgesic market in 1982, and an estimated 7% of J&amp;J's worldwide sales and 15% to 20% of its profits in 1981; the New Mexico case study: 37%). Fortune ties the nationwide capsule recall to the California incident, and reports that company surveys found 94% of consumers aware of the poisonings, 87% of Tylenol users saying the maker was not responsible, and 61% saying they were unlikely to buy Extra-Strength capsules in future <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The company faced a choice between a narrow response (withdrawal in Chicago, warnings) that would limit cost and a broad one (a national recall) that would cost far more and remove any chance that another person would be harmed. As an observable problem: &quot;Seven people had died, the cause was unknown to the public, and further harm was possible.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, using the values frame (FW-C31-08).</em></p>\n<p><em>Facts first.</em> The company did not wait for certainty about the source of the problem; it acted on the risk to people while investigation went on, and it cooperated with authorities and the press.</p>\n<p><em>Stakeholders and harms.</em> The primary stakeholders were customers who could be harmed by an irreversible event (death), then retailers, employees and shareholders. The harm to customers was severe and irreversible; the harm to the company, though large, was financial and recoverable.</p>\n<p><em>The pressure and the legitimate goal.</em> The pressure was financial and reputational; the legitimate goal was to protect people and the brand's long-term trust. These were not opposed over the long run, as the later recovery suggests, though no one could know that at the time.</p>\n<p><em>Options beyond comply or refuse.</em> A regional recall; a national recall of capsules only; a national recall of all Tylenol products; a warning without a recall. In the sources read, Burke advocated a national recall of capsules early in the week and the FBI and FDA initially advised against it; the national recall followed the California incident, and a regional recall is not documented <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Principle.</em> The credo of the company, as the case studies describe it, put the people who use its products first. Whether a credo drove the decision or was invoked afterward is debated; Fortune's contemporaneous account, which quotes Burke at length, does not mention it, and the sources do not settle the question.</p>\n<p><em>Action and communication.</em> Public warnings, daily updates to the media, quick work on tamper-resistant packaging and a reintroduction.</p>\n<p><em>The cost accepted.</em> About $100 million pretax in 1982 dollars, an earnings fall and, for a time, the collapse of the brand's share. The recovery was not certain at the time of the decision.</p>\n<p><em>What this does and does not show.</em> It shows a decision with a large and known cost taken for the safety of others, and it shows that actions with real cost can be consistent with long-term interest, though that cannot be promised. It does not show that doing the right thing always pays, and it is the output of a company's judgment with advice and a regulator's agreement, not one person's. For a candidate, the lesson is structural: say what the harm was, what the pressure was, what options there were, what you chose, and what it cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices the company could have faced; not a record of its deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. A regional withdrawal and warnings</td>\n<td>Lower cost; targeted</td>\n<td>Risk of further harm elsewhere; weaker trust if more cases occur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. A national recall of the affected product (capsules) and a rapid move to tamper-resistant packaging</td>\n<td>Safety and trust</td>\n<td>About $100 million; sales collapse; operational effort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. A national recall of all Tylenol products</td>\n<td>Maximum precaution</td>\n<td>Higher cost; loss of the non-affected forms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B gave the strongest assurance at a cost the company could bear; A saved money at the risk of harm and loss of trust; C went beyond what the evidence required.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the company recalled all Tylenol capsules nationwide and reintroduced the product in tamper-resistant packaging.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the recall, the earnings fall, the share collapse and recovery, the packaging innovation and new rules, and the case's later use as a model of crisis response <code>[V: Fortune; UNM case study; FDA]</code>. Families of three of the victims filed lawsuits against Johnson &amp; Johnson in 1983 <code>[V: law-firm page]</code>; the sources read here do not give their outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had more people been harmed after a narrow response, the cost in lives and in the company's standing would have been far larger. A reader should be careful with hindsight: the company's choice looks obvious now and was uncertain and expensive then. The method to carry away for your own values stories: name the harm, the pressure and the legitimate goal behind it, the options, what you chose, what it cost you, and what you would say if it had not worked out.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Twelve questions. Every model answer is an <code>Illustrative</code> composite: the people, products, numbers and events are invented (invented) to show structure, and none may be used as your own experience; your stories must be true. Each model answer is given as the story you would tell, in notes-then-telling form, followed by the signals it gives. Each ends with follow-ups, which here are the probes an interviewer is likely to ask, and answers to them. Company settings are the typical prompts for that company and describe no internal practice. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c31-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C31-01 · Beginner · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A chronological recitation of the résumé.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A two-minute arc with scope, steps with reasons and a link to the role.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Plants three specific proof points with numbers that invite probes, shows a motive supported by evidence, and ends with a hand-over.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>(A composite candidate from operations, to show a different path from Worked example 1.)</em> &quot;I'm an associate on the operations analytics team at a logistics start-up, where I own the weekly reporting on delivery delays for about 12,000 deliveries a week. I got here in three steps. I started in customer support for a food-delivery company, where I noticed the same five complaints every week and began tagging them; that tagging showed that 31% of contacts were about one confusing status message. I wrote up the finding and the product team changed the message, and contacts about it fell by about half. That was the first time I saw how one decision could change a lot of people's day, and it made me want to be the person making it.</p>\n<p>I then moved to analytics, where I built the delay report and found that two warehouses caused 40% of late deliveries; I proposed a change to cutoff times that the operations lead tried, and late deliveries at those sites fell from 14% to 9%. Most recently I've started running small experiments on notification wording with our product team. What I want now is to own a product area, and your team's work on delivery tracking is a problem I know from the support side and from the data side. I'm happy to go deeper on any of those.&quot; About 230 words.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Scope; an arc with reasons; three proof points (the tagging, the delay report, the experiments); a motive with evidence; a hand-over.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> You said you want to own a product area. What makes you think you're ready? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I've owned decisions at small scale: the status message change and the cutoff-time trial were my proposals, and I followed them to a measured result. What I haven't done is carry a roadmap through a quarter of competing priorities, and that's what I want to learn. In the experiments I'm running now, I've started deciding with the product manager what we will and won't test.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Tell me more about the tagging project. What did you personally do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I built the tag list from a sample of 300 contacts, trained two colleagues to use it, tagged about 2,000 contacts over three weeks and wrote the summary. The product manager and a designer rewrote the message; I measured contacts for six weeks after.&quot; (Then continue with numbers and what went wrong: the first tag list had overlapping categories and I had to redo 200 contacts.)</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c31-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C31-02 · Beginner · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Why product management, and why this company and role?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I like solving problems and working with people; your company is the leader in its field.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives evidence for the motive and one or two concrete reasons for the company.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the realities of the work have been understood, connects a specific company fact to a specific personal fact, and offers what the candidate would examine first in the role.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Two things pulled me to product. One: in my current role I kept being the person who found the problem and then had to hand it to someone else to decide, and I realized I wanted the deciding part: what to build, for whom, and what not to build. Two: the work I enjoy most is the unglamorous part, like deciding which of three reasonable requests gets the one engineer. In a recent quarter I had to say no to two stakeholders and wrote them each a short note with the reasoning; I found I liked the discipline of that. I also know what I would be giving up: less time for analysis and more time in conversations I can't always win.</p>\n";
